<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armenians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Armenian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Armenians are a people of the Armenian highlands with their own language and Christian church. Armenian merchants played major roles in long-distance trade, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I</a> resettled them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. Armenians also served as soldiers and officials in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> they were a major community until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of 1915.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armenians.jpg" alt="Armenians" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Crowell-Collier Publishing Company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armenian_ladies.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around and beneath them lived settled farmers and townspeople: Kurds, <b>Armenians</b>, Syriac Christians, Arabs and, after the conquests of the 1460s, the Persian-speaking population of the Iranian plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Armenians</b> trace their state to Urartu, founded in 860 BCE, and to the Kingdom of Armenia that in 301 CE became the first state to adopt Christianity as its official religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>More followed the Byzantine annexation of Greater Armenia in 1045 and its conquest by the Seljuk Turks nineteen years later: after Manzikert in 1071 Malik-Shah's governors laid repressive taxes on the <b>Armenians</b>, who fled west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An estimated 30,000 people were killed and more than a million displaced, and <b>Armenians</b> were left controlling 14–16 percent of Azerbaijani territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian Greeks and <b>Armenians</b> and the Karaite Jews of Chufut-Kale lived under the khans’ rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Greeks dominated the city and the coastal towns, while Laz communities lived in the east and <b>Armenians</b>, Georgians and, increasingly, Turkmen pastoralists lived in the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
